WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.640
The lecture lists several ways to make communication visual, but a list

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.880
does not make them equivalent.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.440
Separate an English-based signing system, a natural signed language, and a cueing

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.710
system that supplements spoken language.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.650
The useful question is what structure or information each one represents.

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:22.245
In the deck’s Signed English example, a lexical sign combines with grammatical

00:00:22.245 --> 00:00:22.645
markers.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:26.495
Consider cook, cooks, cooked, and cooking.

00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:31.905
The base carries the lexical idea; the added marker changes grammatical information.

00:00:32.455 --> 00:00:34.755
Our written pieces illustrate that relationship.

00:00:35.305 --> 00:00:37.445
They are not instructions for producing the signs.

00:00:38.245 --> 00:00:43.665
English-based systems represent English structure rather than becoming American Sign Language.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.460
American Sign Language, or A S L, is an independent language with

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.340
its own vocabulary and syntax.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.660
Facial expression, head tilt, body movement, and eye gaze also carry information.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.880
Do not reduce it to English words placed on the hands.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.580
The lecture’s broad claim about the only recognized language of Deaf culture

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.180
is not treated here as a universal statement about every community.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:14.630
The slide identifies four physical characteristics: hand configuration, movement, location, and orientation.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.670
Notice the separate questions.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.150
What shape is the hand?

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.440
Where is it?

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:21.560
Which way is it oriented?

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.680
How does it move?

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.170
An explanatory model can vary one feature while holding the others steady.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.280
That model does not assign a real sign meaning, and the four-part

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.380
list does not erase facial or other nonmanual information.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.730
Cued Speech has a different purpose.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.010
It supplements speechreading by adding visual information about phonemes that are unclear

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:44.160
on the lips.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.080
Imagine the mouth leaves several possibilities.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:52.770
The supplementary cues help distinguish those possibilities alongside the spoken language.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.440
This is a conceptual explanation, not an invented cue chart, and it

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.320
is not the same system as A S L.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.700
Use three checkpoints when comparing these approaches.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.830
An English-based system represents English structure.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.540
A S L has its own language structure.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.330
Cued Speech supplements visible spoken-language information.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.580
The student deck contains a naming problem for SEE Two and references

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.480
a comparison table that is absent.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.450
Keep those gaps open rather than memorizing a guessed expansion or unseen

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:24.830
table.

